WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.240
 Jose Ensevce, not so fast El Jefe. Jose himself is a 2012 inductee of the Hall

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.000
 of Fame.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.410
 First Hispanic public official in the history of the city of Cleveland. White

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:17.000
 House fellow in the Reagan administration.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.410
 He's practiced law and led the Puerto Rican and broader Hispanic communities

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:21.000
 for decades.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.240
 He has earned the title that his son is called him of El Jefe. So let's get the

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.000
 boss up here in the duck system.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.000
 Yes, I know there's a singer by that name. Alright.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:44.360
 Anyway, I counted a joy and a privilege to introduce my dear friend of more

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.000
 than 60 years.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.000
 You heard that right, more than 60 years, Sister Alicia Alvarado.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.750
 I'm happy to do that on behalf of our community, especially my organization in

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.000
 Hispanic Roundtable that's sitting over there.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.820
 Well, I'm only going to be able to scratch the surface of her accomplishments

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.000
 and character.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.780
 I'd like to give you a little flavor of the life of this dynamic young Latina,

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.000
 Sister Alicia Alvarado.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
 So let me give you a brief description of what I hope to cover here.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.670
 She's a faithful servant of her God and King. She's the first daughter of the

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.000
 Hispanic community.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:29.000
 Servant leader, grassroots leader, and a friend of all, especially the poor.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.060
 Sister was born in Puerto Rico. She grew up on the Midwest side of Cleveland on

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.000
 the West 31st place.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.000
 She attended St. Patrick's School where she was educated by the personal nuns

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.000
 and influenced by the Jesuit priests.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:51.000
 And that's where she got her initial call to the Sisterhood.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:58.460
 She was the devoted child of her mother, Lorna Rosa, and Don Felipe, and her

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.000
 brothers and sisters, especially her development of the challenge sister.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:08.060
 But her guiding principles and priorities were always God first, her family

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:15.000
 second, the poor, the needy in the weak, and the community were third.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:25.000
 And Alicia was last, always a distant last.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.730
 In our community, she was involved in every, let me underline, every major

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.000
 Hispanic organization and initiative.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.000
 Let me give you a little flavor.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.000
 She began her involvement with the Puerto Rican, well, PR 76.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.260
 And then she went to the Spanish American committee and always was one of the

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.000
 principal leaders there.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:03:05.000
 And she served there for decades, decades, serving the underserved.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:15.000
 Where she always served the underserved for food, housing, and work.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.290
 In 1984, what was the other Hispanic forum, another Hispanic roundtable, she

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:26.200
 helped convene the entirety of our community at San Ignatius High School where

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.000
 we set our first agenda.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.530
 So I remember in 1984, those many late nights in the Justice Center, my former

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:40.440
 office, the city of Cleveland Press Museum's office with Jose Feña Nueva, with

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:46.000
 Jose Feña and Sister Alicia, and trying to plan our event.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.000
 And I can remember always saying to Alicia, how do we know anyone?

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:58.000
 And she always said, she always assured me, she said, my people will come. And

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:58.000
 they did.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.580
 And so at this first event at Ignatius, we had thousands, thousands of people

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:05.000
 that showed up.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:15.330
 And the person that's most responsible for that initial success was sitting

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:18.000
 right over here.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.000
 And they came because Sister Alicia Alvarado asked her to come.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.130
 Because there's no better community organizer in this community because of her

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:32.320
 big love, because of her big heart, and the love of our community for Sister

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:33.000
 Alicia.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:39.800
 The little flavor for this historic accomplishment, just to digress a little

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:46.090
 bit, but among the first things that we did there was that Alicia was a big

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:50.130
 supporter of this, was the Hispanic scholarship forum, which is Esperanza,

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:53.000
 which today gives more than a hundred scholarships.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:58.580
 And it is one of the largest Hispanic scholarship programs and most successful

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:03.990
 in this country, and the person that has made as big a contribution as anyone

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:06.000
 to sit right over there.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:16.000
 So that community participation tradition exists to this very day.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.900
 And I'll explain to you in our last convention recording, we had 3,000 people.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.000
 3,000 people attended.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.730
 And that, approximately the cause of that, is sitting right over there. I mean,

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:29.000
 that is such a big deal.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:35.130
 Did you know who goes there? Dick Pomer! Pomer goes over there. That's how big

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:36.000
 that is.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:42.240
 Anyway, Sister Alicia loves her culture, and I'm reminded of the Puerto Rican

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:48.480
 composer, roughly every night, as we said, "If I had not been born Puerto Rican,

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.000
 I would have wished I had been born Puerto Rican."

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.000
 Anyway, that's Sister Alicia. And it's a core sentiment in Alicia's life.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.000
 So she loves our food, the rice and beans, the bienvene, the roc

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:06.000
 compoi, or the patina, the flan, the pada deja, the marcida, and a sofada. That's

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:06.000
 just where she started.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:14.090
 But, what that has to be associated with music, principally in berengue and

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:17.000
 salsa, and dancing.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:23.690
 And boy, can that girl dance. So you've heard of the flying nun and the singing

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:24.000
 nun,

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:34.000
 but you're shortly going to meet a dancing nun.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.000
 She's always made a profound sense of social justice, and that was manifested

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:43.000
 with her work with the beloved and historic Cesar Childress.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:51.310
 And his efforts and their efforts to, on behalf of Farmworker Movement, to help

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:54.000
 them attain a decent life for all farmworkers throughout America.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:02.000
 When Chavez was here, Sister drove him throughout the entire state, and she was

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:07.000
 Cesar Chavez's chauffeur.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:15.000
 And Cesar Chavez used to refer to her as his bodyguard here in Ohio.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:19.220
 And she played a significant role in people politics. She was intimately

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:23.000
 involved in Charles Stokes's two elections.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:27.220
 Stokes, of course, was the first African American mayor of a major city in

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:28.000
 America.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.410
 Moreover, she campaigned and worked, and principally was responsible for the

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:34.000
 election of Joe Santiago,

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:38.000
 who served admirably as his administrative assistant.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:43.460
 But she also shared her gifts with her American sisters, where she served on

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:46.000
 their national board as well.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.140
 And that is as a result of her humility, her leadership gifts, and her love of

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:52.000
 the Lord.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:58.120
 About ten years ago, she suffered a massive stroke, and I remember seeing her

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:02.000
 at her rehab facility in the fetal position,

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:07.220
 and unable to speak. And truthfully, I did not believe that she was going to

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:08.000
 survive.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:13.000
 But there are modern day miracles, and God in His goodness gave her back to us

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:17.000
 because He still had more for her to do.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:22.040
 But let me finish with a couple of scripture quotes that come to mind when I

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:24.000
 think of Sister Alicia.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:33.210
 The first is in the Old Testament, to do it around me. "You shall walk after

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:37.000
 the Lord your God, and you shall serve Him and hold fast to Him."

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:41.000
 And from my end of the Testament, James 2.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:47.080
 "But what good is it, my brothers, if someone says that He has faith, but He

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:49.000
 does not have works?

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:54.300
 If your brother or sister is poorly clothed and lacking in daily food, and one

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.530
 of you says, 'Then go and peace be warmed and filled without giving them the

00:08:57.530 --> 00:09:02.000
 things needed for the body,' what good is that?"

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:08.010
 God has a special focus on the poor, the words of the evangelist Luke,

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:12.000
 regarding principles in Sister Alicia's life.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:19.720
 "What you've done for the least of my brethren, you've done for me." So she has

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:24.000
 lived her faith and served our God by serving others.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:31.130
 So let me give you this great servant leader, this patron saint of the Hispanic

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:38.000
 community, this bodyguard, this friend of all, especially the weak and the poor,

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:43.000
 the dancing nun, Elmara Alicia Barad.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:20.000
 [applause]

